<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johor Bahru</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tanjung Puteri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> Bahru is the capital of the Malaysian state of Johor, at the southern tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> across the strait from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. Founded in 1855 as Tanjung Puteri by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temenggong-daeng-ibrahim">Temenggong Daeng Ibrahim</a>, it was renamed and made the capital by his son Abu Bakar in the 1860s. A causeway opened in 1924 links it to Singapore.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johor-bahru.jpg" alt="Johor Bahru" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R&amp;F Malaysia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%AF%8C%E5%8A%9B%E5%85%AC%E4%B8%BB%E6%B9%BE_R%26F_Princess_Cove_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Johor Bahru lies along the Straits of Johor at the southern end of Peninsular Malaysia, on mostly flat land with some hills, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. Its climate is equatorial, with steady temperatures, high humidity and an average annual rainfall of around 2,350 mm, and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> periods each year. The name means New Johor in Malay, distinguishing it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-lama">Johor Lama</a>, Old Johor. The area was originally Tanjung Puteri, a Malay fishing village, and the city has also been spelt Johore Bahru, Johor Baru or Johor Baharu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1819 a dispute between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> split the Johor-Riau Sultanate: the mainland portion came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temenggong-daeng-ibrahim">Temenggong Daeng Ibrahim</a>, and the Riau-Lingga Sultanate under the Bugis. The Temenggong already had a close relationship with the British, who wanted to control trade at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. By a treaty signed in Singapore on 10 March 1855, Sultan Ali was to be crowned Sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> and receive 5,000 Spanish dollars and an allowance of 500 a month, and in return ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of Johor, except Kesang in Muar, to the Temenggong. The Temenggong renamed the territory Iskandar Puteri and at first administered it from Telok Blangah in Singapore, then, after he arrived in 1858, from the new settlement.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor">Sultan Abu Bakar</a> the town was modernised from a fishing village, with administrative centres, offices, schools and a railway to Woodlands. The State Mosque and Royal Palace were built with the aid of Wong Ah Fook, a patron of the sultan. Abu Bakar set up his administration under a British style and a state constitution, the Undang-undang Tubuh Negeri Johor. Although the British had long been advisers to the sultanate, it did not come under direct colonial rule until the adviser's status was raised to that of a Resident, as in the Federated Malay States, under Sultan Ibrahim in 1914.</p><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomoyuki-yamashita">Tomoyuki Yamashita</a>'s Japanese forces invaded the town on 31 January 1942. The British retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and blew up the causeway the day before. The Japanese used the sultan's Bukit Serene Palace as their base, aware that the British would not attack it. After the war Johor Bahru became a birthplace of Malay nationalism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">UMNO</a> was formed at the Istana Besar in 1946. The town remained the state capital after Malaysia's formation in 1963 and was granted city status on 1 January 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Johor Bahru is the capital of the Malaysian state of Johor and had a population of 858,118 in the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its people are mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, Chinese and Indians, and it is part of Iskandar Malaysia. Its cultural sites include the Royal Abu Bakar Museum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a> building and the Johor Bahru Chinese Heritage Museum, and the main football club is Johor Darul Ta'zim.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johor Bahru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Abu Bakar, Johor became a model of a modernising Malay monarchy, and in 1946 <b>Johor Bahru</b> was the birthplace of the United Malays National Organisation (UMNO), founded under the Johor chief minister Onn Jaafar to oppose the Malayan Union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
